# B6 bounded IdealFrontierSim machine — work in progress

This ignored scratch project is building the entire mathematical public
simulator in explicit stages. Completed stages do not yet establish a complete
resource bound for the full program. It is not a deployed Rust simulator.
No production gate, frozen argument, accepted primitive premise, or previous
evidence is modified. **Full coverage and qualification remain false.**

The exact coverage registry is `coverage.json`. Its source hashes are a selected
correspondence inventory, not an automatic Rust semantic proof or full build
dependency closure. An unresolved stage remains unresolved even when the IR
compiler and all completed stages pass their own tests.

## Boundary and invariants

The intended entry is the frozen `IdealFrontierSim` proposal in
`specs/parliament_ballot_b6_ideal_public_sampler.md`, section 6: valid bounded
typed public requests, candidate/comparator rows, registration/vote roles,
vote list size 3 through 1,000, and classical atomic requests. The machine
must generate the same public proof or exhaustion error and the same
chronological programming history. No adversary acts inside one request.
Malformed raw envelopes, arbitrary governance-preimage construction, and a
new superposition proof-request API are not this entry.

The fixed base backend function uses a response-XOR oracle. Its full message
register, namespace/domain encoding, response padding and explicit finite
length width follow the frozen oracle-log proposal. Programming means explicit
log-state updates, not changing an invisible external function. Every coherent
lookup reads the whole immutable base input and the current chronological log.
The existing construction's two unconditional base calls are charged as two,
including any reverse traversal added by a surrounding clean embedding.

Fresh sampler bits are independent input registers with their preparation and
retained size counted separately. Classical sampling is a mixture of those
basis states. Retaining old inputs/coins/private history is permissible only
after proving equality of the public projection. A unitary cannot erase an
unknown register for free. Likewise, clean input/output registers and disjoint
wire regions are explicit preconditions; primitive outputs are never supplied
for free by calling them an interface.

